{
    "title": "Increasing Medical Marijuana and Cannabidiol Research",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 23, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-23/pdf/2025-23846.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House just issued an order to speed up research on medical marijuana and CBD and to move marijuana to a less restrictive federal category to make studies and patient access easier. It also asks Congress to set clearer rules for hemp-based products so consumers know what they\u2019re getting and doctors have better guidance.\n\nWhy now? Many states already allow medical marijuana, and federal health agencies have found credible evidence that it can help with pain, nausea and vomiting from chemotherapy, and anorexia tied to certain illnesses. Millions of patients, including many seniors and veterans, are using these products, and some report using fewer opioids. But decades of strict federal rules have made it hard to study marijuana properly, leaving doctors and patients without clear, consistent advice. CBD is popular too, yet many products are mislabeled or confusing, and some will soon fall under tighter control because of their THC content.\n\nWhat changes? The Attorney General is directed to finish the process of moving marijuana to a category that recognizes medical use and lowers research barriers. Congress is being urged to define hemp-derived products more clearly and set guardrails, such as limits on THC per serving and per container, and reasonable ratios of CBD to THC. Health agencies are tasked with building research models that use real-world data to quickly understand benefits and risks, especially for young people and other vulnerable groups, and to inform standards of care.\n\nWho stands to benefit? Patients living with chronic pain, cancer-related symptoms, or appetite loss; seniors and veterans seeking alternatives; doctors who need evidence to guide treatment; researchers and responsible manufacturers; and consumers who want accurate labels and safer products.\n\nWho could be negatively affected? Parts of the CBD and hemp market that rely on loose rules may face stricter testing, labeling, and THC limits, adding costs or reducing product lines. There may be short-term uncertainty as rules change and federal and state policies align. Some people worry about increased youth access or dependence, and tighter standards could limit availability of certain products that users prefer today. Rescheduling does not legalize marijuana for general use, which may disappoint those expecting broader changes.\n\nOverall, the order aims to match real-world use with rigorous science and clearer standards, balancing access with safety.",
